<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · siege</p><h1 class="enc-title">Siege of Leningrad</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1941">1941</a>–<a href="/years/1944">1944</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The siege of Leningrad was a military <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> of the Soviet city of Leningrad, present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a>, by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> on the Eastern Front of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a>; it lasted 872 days, and the city was never captured. Land routes to the rest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> were cut on 8 September 1941, and the Germans decided to bomb the city and starve its inhabitants. Many residents starved during the winter of 1941–1942; the siege caused an estimated 1.5 million deaths, from a prewar population of 3.2 million, and some historians have classified it as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a>. Supplies came across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-ladoga">Lake Ladoga</a>, in winter over the Road of Life on the ice, until the siege was fully broken on 27 January 1944.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/siege-of-leningrad.jpg" alt="Siege of Leningrad" width="330" height="242" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Boris Kudoyarov · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anti_aircraft_Leningrad_1941.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The capture of Leningrad was one of three strategic goals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/operation-barbarossa">Operation Barbarossa</a> and the main target of Army Group North under Field Marshal Wilhelm Ritter von Leeb. The city was the former capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, the birthplace of the Russian Revolution, the main base of the Soviet Baltic Fleet and, in 1939, the source of 11% of Soviet industrial output. On 6 August 1941 Hitler repeated his order: &quot;Leningrad first, Donetsk Basin second, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> third.&quot; In the city, more than a million citizens were mobilised to build fortifications, and some 1,743,129 people, including 414,148 children, were evacuated between 29 June 1941 and 31 March 1943.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The Germans severed the last rail connection on 30 August and on 8 September reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-ladoga">Lake Ladoga</a> at Shlisselburg, leaving only a corridor between the lake and the city. Finnish forces came within 20 km of the northern suburbs. Leningrad was supplied over Lake Ladoga by boat in summer and in winter over the ice, a route that opened on 20 November and became the Road of Life. The winter of 1941–1942 killed many by starvation, and reports of cannibalism appeared, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nkvd">NKVD</a> records on the subject were not published until 2004.</p><p>Operation Iskra began on 12 January 1943, and on 18 January units of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volkhov-river">Volkhov</a> and Leningrad Fronts met, opening a land corridor about 8 miles wide. The siege ended on 27 January 1944, when the Leningrad–Novgorod offensive drove German forces 60 to 100 km from the city. Before retreating, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wehrmacht">Wehrmacht</a>, on Hitler's orders, looted and destroyed palaces such as Peterhof and the Catherine Palace.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>During the siege some 3,200 residential buildings and 840 factories and plants were destroyed. The judges at the High Command trial ruled that cutting off sustenance from a besieged city was legitimate under the law as it then stood. Some twenty-first-century historians, among them Timo Vihavainen and Nikita Lomagin, classify the siege as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a>. Almost all Finnish historians regard it as a German operation, while the Russian historian Nikolai Baryshnikov argues that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> took an active part. A military parade is held on Palace Square each 27 January.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Siege of Leningrad” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
